- Stating that the "JVM is platform-independent". The JVM is platform-dependent; it is the bytecode that is platform-independent.
- Assuming platform independence means code can run without installing Java (a compatible JVM runtime is required).

### Comparison Reference
| Dimension | C / C++ Compilation | Java Compilation |
|---|---|---|
| **Compilation Output** | Native binary machine code directly | Intermediate bytecode (.class) |
| **Portability** | Source-level only (must recompile per OS) | Binary-level (same .class runs on all OSs) |
| **Intermediate Layer** | None | Java Virtual Machine (JVM) |
| **File Extension** | `.exe`, `.out`, `.dll`, `.so` | `.class`, `.jar` |', 'In interviews, highlight the non-obvious nuances: - "Java is platform-independent because its compiler (javac) does not generate target machine code directly. Instead, it generates platform-neutral intermediate bytecode."
