UPDATE notes SET
  title = 'Garbage Collection & finalize() Method Notes',
  definition = '- **Definition**: **Garbage Collection (GC)** is the automatic memory management process in Java where the JVM identifies and reclaims heap memory occupied by unreferenced objects.
- **The `finalize()` Method**: A legacy protected method in `java.lang.Object` intended for cleanup before GC; **officially DEPRECATED in Java 9 and marked for removal in Java 18+**.',
  simple_explanation = '- **The City Recycling Truck Analogy**: In C++, you must walk your household trash to the landfill yourself (`free()`). In Java, a city recycling truck (Garbage Collector) drives through the neighborhood periodically. If a box has no mailbox label attached (no active live references), the truck loads and recycles it automatically.',
  why_needed = '',
  how_it_works = '',
  important_concepts = '- **When is an Object Eligible for Garbage Collection?**:
  • An object is eligible when it is **no longer reachable** from any live GC Root (active thread stacks, static references, JNI handles).
  • 4 Ways to Make an Object Eligible:
    1. Nullifying the reference variable: `obj = null;`.
    2. Reassigning reference to another object: `obj1 = obj2;`.
    3. Object created inside a method going out of scope.
    4. Island of Isolation: Objects referencing each other but isolated from GC roots.
- **Why `finalize()` is Dangerous and Deprecated**:
  • No execution guarantee: JVM does not guarantee when or if `finalize()` will run.
  • Performance killer: Prolongs object lifetime by at least one full GC cycle.
  • Modern Replacement: **`AutoCloseable`** with **try-with-resources** or `java.lang.ref.Cleaner`.
- **Can we force Garbage Collection?**:
  • `System.gc()` or `Runtime.getRuntime().gc()` only **requests/suggests** GC to the JVM. The JVM is not obligated to execute it immediately!',
  real_world_examples = '',
  java_code_examples = 'public class GarbageCollectionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String data = new String("Temporary Data");
        // Nullifying reference makes the object eligible for GC
        data = null; 

        // Suggesting GC execution (advisory only)
        System.gc();
        System.out.println("Requested Garbage Collection cycle.");
    }
}

### Output & Execution Mechanics
- Demonstrates reference nullification and triggering advisory garbage collection via `System.gc()`.',
  internal_working = '',
  advantages_disadvantages = '',
  important_differences = '',
  common_tricky_questions = '- **What is an Island of Isolation?** When Object A references Object B, and Object B references Object A, but neither is referenced by any live GC root. The JVM detects this circular loop and reclaims both objects!',
  interview_ready_answer = '- "Java Garbage Collection automatically reclaims heap memory from objects that are no longer reachable from any GC Root."
- "System.gc() is strictly an advisory request, not a guarantee. The finalize() method is deprecated due to non-deterministic execution and resource leaks, replaced by AutoCloseable and try-with-resources."',
  quick_revision_points = '- Automatic heap memory reclamation.
- Eligible when unreachable from GC Roots.
- `System.gc()` is a request, NOT a guarantee.
- `finalize()` is deprecated; use try-with-resources.',
