- "It provides modularity, security, and maintainability for large-scale enterprise systems."', '- **The Automobile Analogy**:
  • **Encapsulation**: The internal engine and transmission are sealed under the hood; you cannot tamper with spark plugs while driving.
  • **Inheritance**: A Tesla Model 3 inherits wheels, headlights, and brakes from the generic Vehicle blueprint.
  • **Polymorphism**: Pressing the accelerator pedal makes an electric car spin a motor and a gas car inject fuel, but the driver calls the same pedal action.
  • **Abstraction**: You steer the wheel and press the pedals without needing to know fuel-injection physics or battery chemistry.', '4 Pillars: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction.'),
(92, 1, 'Intermediate', 2, 1, 1, 'How does Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) operate in practical Java applications, and what architectural rules must be followed?', 'Operational mechanics of Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).', '

// Modeling an OOP entity
public class OopPillarsDemo {
    // Encapsulation: Private state
    private String model;
    private int speed;

    public OopPillarsDemo(String model) {
        this.model = model;
        this.speed = 0;
    }

    // Abstraction & Behavior
    public void accelerate(int delta) {
        if (delta > 0) this.speed += delta;
        System.out.println(model + " accelerating. Current speed: " + speed + " km/h");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        OopPillarsDemo car = new OopPillarsDemo("Tesla Model S");
        car.accelerate(40);
    }
}

### Output & Execution Mechanics
- Demonstrates an encapsulated object maintaining internal state and exposing validated public behavior.', 'In production Java systems, Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is used to guarantee reliability and performance. Key architectural considerations include proper design patterns, memory awareness, and adhering to language specifications.', 'Refer to standard code example demonstrating proper syntax and execution.', 'Contrasts with procedural languages by prioritizing data security and modularity.'),
(92, 1, 'Advanced', 3, 1, 1, 'What tricky edge cases, common pitfalls, or interview traps are associated with Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)?', 'Be cautious with null handling, type compatibility, and concurrency rules with Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).', '| Dimension | Procedural Programming (C, Pascal) | Object-Oriented Programming (Java, C++) |
|---|---|---|
| **Primary Focus** | Step-by-step algorithms and functions | Objects modeling real-world data and behavior |
| **Data Security** | Low; data moves freely across functions | High; data is encapsulated inside private classes |
| **Code Reusability** | Limited (copy-pasting or function calls) | High (Inheritance, Polymorphism, Composition) |
| **Approach** | Top-down decomposition | Bottom-up design |

### Comparison Reference
| Dimension | Procedural Programming (C, Pascal) | Object-Oriented Programming (Java, C++) |
|---|---|---|
| **Primary Focus** | Step-by-step algorithms and functions | Objects modeling real-world data and behavior |
| **Data Security** | Low; data moves freely across functions | High; data is encapsulated inside private classes |
| **Code Reusability** | Limited (copy-pasting or function calls) | High (Inheritance, Polymorphism, Composition) |
| **Approach** | Top-down decomposition | Bottom-up design |', 'In interviews, highlight the non-obvious nuances: - "OOP is a paradigm organizing software around objects modeling state and behavior via four pillars: Encapsulation (data hiding), Inheritance (code reuse), Polymorphism (multiple forms), and Abstraction (complexity hiding)."
- "It provides modularity, security, and maintainability for large-scale enterprise systems."', 'Production edge-case scenarios and code maintenance.', 'Objects combine state (fields) and behavior (methods).');

