    String species;
    public Bird(String species) { this.species = species; }
    void breathe() { System.out.println(species + " is breathing oxygen."); }
}

// Sparrow IS-A Bird and CAN-DO Flyable
class Sparrow extends Bird implements Flyable {
    public Sparrow() { super("House Sparrow"); }

    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println(species + " is flying high in the sky!");
    }
}

public class ComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Sparrow s = new Sparrow();
        s.breathe();
        s.fly();
    }
}

### Output & Execution Mechanics
- Sparrow inherits shared biological state (`species`, `breathe`) from `Bird` and fulfills the flight capability contract via `Flyable`.', 'In production Java systems, Interface vs Abstract Class (Architectural Comparison) is used to guarantee reliability and performance. Key architectural considerations include proper design patterns, memory awareness, and adhering to language specifications.', 'Refer to standard code example demonstrating proper syntax and execution.', 'Interface = CAN-DO, no constructors, only constants and public contracts, multiple inheritance.'),
(108, 1, 'Advanced', 3, 1, 1, 'What tricky edge cases, common pitfalls, or interview traps are associated with Interface vs Abstract Class (Architectural Comparison)?', 'Be cautious with null handling, type compatibility, and concurrency rules with Interface vs Abstract Class (Architectural Comparison).', '| Dimension | Abstract Class | Interface |
|---|---|---|
| **Inheritance Support** | Single class inheritance (`extends`) | Multiple interface inheritance (`implements`) |
| **State / Variables** | Can have instance variables, final, and non-final fields | Only `public static final` constants |
| **Constructors** | Supported (called via `super()`) | No constructors allowed |
| **Method Implementation** | Can have any mix of abstract and concrete methods | Abstract by default; can have `default` and `static` (Java 8+), `private` (Java 9+) |
| **Speed / Performance** | Faster; direct virtual method lookup | Slightly slower; dynamic search across interface tables (itable) |
| **Relationship** | IS-A relationship (e.g., Dog is an Animal) | CAN-DO capability (e.g., Bird, Airplane can Fly -> `Flyable`) |
| **Access Modifiers** | Can have `private`, `protected`, and `public` members | All members are `public` by default |

### Comparison Reference
| Dimension | Abstract Class | Interface |
|---|---|---|
| **Inheritance Support** | Single class inheritance (`extends`) | Multiple interface inheritance (`implements`) |
| **State / Variables** | Can have instance variables, final, and non-final fields | Only `public static final` constants |
| **Constructors** | Supported (called via `super()`) | No constructors allowed |
| **Method Implementation** | Can have any mix of abstract and concrete methods | Abstract by default; can have `default` and `static` (Java 8+), `private` (Java 9+) |
| **Speed / Performance** | Faster; direct virtual method lookup | Slightly slower; dynamic search across interface tables (itable) |
| **Relationship** | IS-A relationship (e.g., Dog is an Animal) | CAN-DO capability (e.g., Bird, Airplane can Fly -> `Flyable`) |
| **Access Modifiers** | Can have `private`, `protected`, and `public` members | All members are `public` by default |', 'In interviews, highlight the non-obvious nuances: - "Use an abstract class when classes share code, state, or constructors in a strict IS-A hierarchy."
- "Use an interface to define a common capability contract across unrelated classes or to enable multiple inheritance of behavior."', 'Production edge-case scenarios and code maintenance.', 'Prefer interfaces for APIs and loose coupling.');

