public class StackFrameDemo {
    public static int calculate(int a, int b) {
        // Pushes frame \'calculate\' on top of \'main\'
        // Local variables a, b, result stored in calculate\'s LVT
        int result = a * b;
        return result; // Frame popped off, return value delivered to caller
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Frame \'main\' pushed onto main thread\'s Stack
        int x = 10;
        int y = 20;
        int sum = calculate(x, y);
        System.out.println("Result: " + sum);
    }
}

### Output & Execution Mechanics
- Method calls push frames; method completions pop frames. Memory deallocation is instant without any Garbage Collector intervention.', 'In production Java systems, Stack Memory & Stack Frames Structure is used to guarantee reliability and performance. Key architectural considerations include proper design patterns, memory awareness, and adhering to language specifications.', 'Refer to standard code example demonstrating proper syntax and execution.', 'Frame components: Local Variable Table, Operand Stack, Frame Data.'),
(112, 1, 'Advanced', 3, 1, 1, 'What tricky edge cases, common pitfalls, or interview traps are associated with Stack Memory & Stack Frames Structure?', '### Common Pitfalls & Mistakes', '### Common Pitfalls & Mistakes
- Believing objects live on the Stack: only primitive values and object references live on the Stack; the actual objects always live on the Heap (unless optimized by Escape Analysis in modern JIT).', 'In interviews, highlight the non-obvious nuances: - "Each Java thread has its own private Stack. Each method call pushes a Stack Frame containing a Local Variable Table, an Operand Stack, and Frame Data."
- "When the method terminates, its frame is immediately popped off. Excessive recursion causes StackOverflowError."', 'Production edge-case scenarios and code maintenance.', 'Deallocation is instantaneous upon method return.');

