  • Extend **`java.lang.Exception`** to create a **Custom Checked Exception** (caller must handle or declare).
  • Extend **`java.lang.RuntimeException`** to create a **Custom Unchecked Exception** (standard for modern APIs and Spring microservices).

- Standard Java exceptions like `IllegalArgumentException` are generic. In a banking application, throwing `InsufficientFundsException` with the current balance and attempted withdrawal amount communicates the exact domain failure instantly to API consumers.', '- "Custom exceptions are created by extending Exception (for checked) or RuntimeException (for unchecked) to provide domain-specific error handling."
- "Modern Java and Spring applications prefer extending RuntimeException to avoid verbose boilerplate and integrate with global exception handlers like @ControllerAdvice."', '- Standard Java exceptions like `IllegalArgumentException` are generic. In a banking application, throwing `InsufficientFundsException` with the current balance and attempted withdrawal amount communicates the exact domain failure instantly to API consumers.', 'Extend `Exception` for checked custom exceptions.'),
(129, 1, 'Intermediate', 2, 1, 1, 'How does Custom / User-Defined Exceptions Creation operate in practical Java applications, and what architectural rules must be followed?', 'Operational mechanics of Custom / User-Defined Exceptions Creation.', '### Syntax & Core Structure
```java
// Checked Custom Exception
public class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {
    public InsufficientFundsException(String msg) { super(msg); }
}

// Unchecked Custom Exception (Recommended in modern Spring Boot)
public class ResourceNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public ResourceNotFoundException(String msg) { super(msg); }
}
```

// Domain-specific custom unchecked exception
class InsufficientBalanceException extends RuntimeException {
    private final double requested;
    private final double available;

    public InsufficientBalanceException(double requested, double available) {
        super(String.format("Withdrawal of $%.2f failed! Available balance is only $%.2f.", requested, available));
        this.requested = requested;
        this.available = available;
    }

    public double getRequested() { return requested; }
    public double getAvailable() { return available; }
}

class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(double balance) { this.balance = balance; }

    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > balance) {
            throw new InsufficientBalanceException(amount, balance);
        }
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrew $" + amount + ". Remaining: $" + balance);
    }
}

public class CustomExceptionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankAccount acc = new BankAccount(500.0);
        try {
            acc.withdraw(800.0);
        } catch (InsufficientBalanceException ex) {
            System.err.println("Transaction Blocked: " + ex.getMessage());
            System.err.println("Shortage: $" + (ex.getRequested() - ex.getAvailable()));
        }
    }
}

### Output & Execution Mechanics
- Illustrates how custom exceptions carry rich domain-specific state and provide expressive error diagnostics.', 'In production Java systems, Custom / User-Defined Exceptions Creation is used to guarantee reliability and performance. Key architectural considerations include proper design patterns, memory awareness, and adhering to language specifications.', 'Refer to standard code example demonstrating proper syntax and execution.', 'Extend `RuntimeException` for unchecked custom exceptions.'),
(129, 1, 'Advanced', 3, 1, 1, 'What tricky edge cases, common pitfalls, or interview traps are associated with Custom / User-Defined Exceptions Creation?', 'Be cautious with null handling, type compatibility, and concurrency rules with Custom / User-Defined Exceptions Creation.', '', 'In interviews, highlight the non-obvious nuances: - "Custom exceptions are created by extending Exception (for checked) or RuntimeException (for unchecked) to provide domain-specific error handling."
- "Modern Java and Spring applications prefer extending RuntimeException to avoid verbose boilerplate and integrate with global exception handlers like @ControllerAdvice."', 'Production edge-case scenarios and code maintenance.', 'Modern frameworks favor unchecked custom exceptions.');

