- Calling **`iterator.remove()`** safely removes the element from the backing data structure AND synchronizes the iterator\'s expected `expectedModCount = modCount`.

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;

public class SafeRemovalDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(10, 15, 20, 25, 30));

        // SAFE: Using Iterator\'s remove() method
        Iterator<Integer> it = numbers.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            Integer val = it.next();
            if (val % 2 != 0) {
                it.remove(); // Safely removes odd numbers!
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Remaining even numbers: " + numbers);

        // Modern Java 8+ equivalent:
        numbers.removeIf(n -> n > 25);
        System.out.println("After removeIf (>25): " + numbers);
    }
}

### Output & Execution Mechanics
- Removes odd numbers without triggering `ConcurrentModificationException`.
- Highlights Java 8 `removeIf()` as the modern syntactic shorthand.', 'In production Java systems, Iterator Cursor & Safe Element Removal is used to guarantee reliability and performance. Key architectural considerations include proper design patterns, memory awareness, and adhering to language specifications.', 'Refer to standard code example demonstrating proper syntax and execution.', 'Methods: `hasNext()`, `next()`, and `remove()`.'),
